{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UiehoL8xNZQJUwUaMr3VvW","type":"tool_result","content":"===== specification/PRODUCT.md =====\n# STDO Skills\n\nStatus: common construction candidate under T-001; not Product acceptance or release.\n\n## Product boundary\n\nProduct identity: `urn:stdo:product-definition:stdo-skills`.\nBounded context: `urn:stdo-skills:bounded-context:agent-method-application`.\n\nThe Product supplies skills for F_P agents applying an exactly selected STDO\nmethodology to software-development work. It connects recognizable user tasks\nto the deciding source, appropriate context, supported tool operations and\nobservable returns. It owns requirements for this user-facing capability and\nevidence that each tenant realizes it.\n\nSTDO owns the method. a_c and other declared Development Products own their tool\ncontracts and computational implementations. A skill preserves and applies those\ncontracts; it does not create another lifecycle, state store or source of\nassessment, admission, acceptance or publication authority.\n\n## Selected methodology\n\nThe complete static builder and initial supported methodology is\n`stdo://releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/`, manifest\n`60356364a412dbde8cffe68fe0eda4e2749b0162acce93495534eee13b223df8`.\nNative commit: `fe4941f56a52ed3697a2b516d8d68948b0ea65d9`;\npackage: `specification_methodology/v3`.\nThe [Product Definition](../stdo_skills.json) owns the operative locator.\nThe mutable `3.0.1` discovery channel is not an adoption instruction.\n\nThe release remains static while this Product's requirements, designs and skills\nare developed. A later method adoption requires an explicit compatibility\ndecision and affected qualification; an implementation defect does not authorize\nediting the selected methodology.\n\n## Users and capability\n\nThe user works in a governed workspace using Codex or Claude. Skills support:\n\n- setup and exact Product/method selection;\n- intake and re-entry at the changed requirement, design or realization owner;\n- context acquisition and bounded frame construction;\n- authorized direct work or Executive-directed delegation;\n- source-first independent review where required;\n- continuation, recovery, currentness and truthful status;\n- release preparation and separately authorized release operations.\n\nThe shared requirements state outcomes and discriminators. They do not prescribe\nskill count, names, prompt identity, invocation syntax or host-specific mechanism.\nEach tenant owns its computational and execution binding, including instructions,\nsupporting references, tool integration, installation and tuning.\n\n## Tool relationship\n\nThe current `stdo` manager can verify and resolve the exact installation. Existing\na_c mechanics are available through an explicitly selected implementation and\nmethod binding. The current legacy lifecycle profile is not renamed to 3.0.1.\nEach operation used for a 3.0.1 claim needs established compatibility or a declared\nsupported translation. Reading opaque source bytes under an honestly identified\nlegacy acquisition contract makes only that acquisition claim.\n\nTool harmonisation remains owned by a_c. This Product may identify required\ncontracts, report defects and consume a supported increment; it does not fork\ntool semantics into each tenant. A skill's direct use of host facilities must\nbe declared in its design, preserve the selected operation contract and be\nqualified on that actual path. It cannot substitute for a required tool result.\n\nF_D classification follows the selected calculus's totality and dependency\nrequirements. An F_D constructor gains no assessment or admission authority from\nthat classification; invoking an F_P actor does not make its semantic answer\ndeterministic. The deciding owners are in [SOURCE_OWNERS.md](SOURCE_OWNERS.md).\n\n## Independent tenants and releases\n\n`build_tenants/codex/` and `build_tenants/claude/` independently realize the common\nWHAT. Evidence and supported-host claims are tenant-specific. Both use the same\ntask semantics and acceptance predicates, while host realization and tuning may\ndiffer materially. Common machinery is reused where its contract fits; retained\nvariants need a concrete supported difference.\n\nThis Product is a peer at the monorepo root. It has its own lifecycle and release\nidentity and is not automatically a member of an existing STDO release cohort.\nThe present work constructs candidates and performs bounded qualification; it\ndoes not publish a release or install skills into unrelated user environments.\n\n## Construction authority\n\nJim owns Product scope, common meaning, acceptance and release. His recorded\n[T-001 instruction](../.ai-workspace/tickets/active/T-001-skills-dogfood.md#owner-instruction)\nselects this Product, its two tenants, static method and Executive-directed\nconstruction. Root is the bounded Executive for that work and can select\nrequirements/design candidates for construction, allocate exact Writer effects,\nevaluate returns, request supported repairs and retain residuals within that\noutcome. It cannot silently broaden Product meaning or claim owner acceptance.\n\nThe current [frame basis](REFERENCE_FRAME_BASIS.md) is proposed for this\nconstruction. Its disposition must be recorded against exact candidate bytes.\nThe owner's work authorization, a drafted execution contract, deterministic\nadmission and acceptance remain distinct. T-001 records the initial construction\ninterpretation and the unresolved integration of later admission contracts.\n\n## Acceptance boundary\n\nInitial delivery requires a coherent common specification, both realized tenant\ndesigns, usable host installation paths, supported tool bindings, and independent\nassessment of the [finite scenario evidence](requirements/SCENARIOS.md).\nMissing or failed mandatory evidence withholds the corresponding supported claim.\nIt cannot be hidden by a suite total or by the other tenant succeeding.\n\nRepeated use may establish broader reliability and benefit. This first experiment\nsupports only the cases, hosts, settings, tool versions and outcomes actually\nobserved. Product acceptance and release remain owner decisions on that evidence.\n\n\n===== specification/INTENT.md =====\n# Intent\n\nMake STDO usable through skills that help agents choose and perform the right\nwork with the available computational tools. Reduce repeated ad hoc context\nconstruction, omitted checks and recovery mistakes while preserving the owner's\noutcome and the methodology's distinctions.\n\nBuild one common Product with independently tuned Codex and Claude realizations.\nKeep the released STDO 3.0.1 methodology static during this experiment. Use the\nmethod and current tools to build the skills, exposing real integration gaps as\nevidence rather than explaining them away.\n\nDemonstrate actual tool use and useful outcomes on declared tasks before claiming\neffective operation. Efficiency improvement and reliable automatic selection are\nempirical claims to measure, not consequences of packaging instructions as skills.\n\n\n===== specification/requirements/SCENARIOS.md =====\n# Common qualification scenarios\n\nThis is the shared oracle specification. Tenant implementations may use different\nskill names and host mechanisms. Execute the same substantive tasks, authorities\nand expected outcomes on both hosts; retain the exact tenant and tool cuts.\n\n## Protocol\n\nFor each case, freeze a small isolated workspace with a Product Definition, the\nselected method, relevant source and an explicit effect grant where needed.\nDerive expected outcomes from the common requirement before acquiring the tenant's\nanswer. Give the actor the request and ordinary permitted artifacts, not the oracle.\nRun automatic-discovery and explicit-invocation lanes separately. Freeze model,\nhost version, settings, source exposure, resource bound and any environmental\ndifferences. A fallback invocation after automatic failure is another attempt.\n\nRecord actual host/tool events, material postimages and the actor's final result.\nAssess the outcome and required path together. Missing traces or unavailable host\ncapability leave that claim unestablished. Every failed attempt remains in the\nrecord. One completed run per mandatory case/host is the minimum finite witness;\nit is not an estimated success rate. Repetitions for reliability or cost comparison\nneed a separately selected sample plan. A larger suite is unnecessary absent a\nnew failure or material change.\n\n## Cases and falsifiers\n\n| Case | Ordinary task and controlled distinction | Required observation and falsifier | Requirements |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Q01 | Ask to perform a bounded STDO workspace change without naming a skill; then run equivalent fresh explicit invocation | Actual skill acquisition and required tool calls lead to the requested change; failure to discover is reported separately from execution failure | SK-01, 03, 04, 12 |\n| Q02 | Ask an unrelated factual or non-governed task | No forced STDO setup, new work carrier or irrelevant tool sequence | SK-01, 03 |\n| Q03 | Resolve a valid pinned workspace; in isolated variants remove the install, alter one installed member, and introduce ambiguous Product selection | Valid case uses exact verified cut; each invalid case withholds only dependent effects and identifies the cause, never substitutes latest or sibling source | SK-02 |\n| Q04 | Authorized one-file correction with sufficient existing requirement/design and direct Writer scope | Correct change and focused evidence without invented mandatory ticket, coordination or review; no write outside scope | SK-03, 06 |\n| Q05 | A change requiring one bounded worker and independent review | Actual handoff preserves basis/grant/return; reviewer acquires deciding sources and exact candidate; Executive consumes results before next activation | SK-05, 06, 07 |\n| Q06 | Present a tool that returns unsupported method and one that returns an execution error | Actual refusal/error retained; no manufactured success, silent bypass or semantic conclusion from transport; next supported re-entry identified | SK-04, 09 |\n| Q07 | Resume two histories with identical candidate bytes: one has a current accepted assessment, one has an unresolved counterexample invalidating that assessment | Continuations differ as required; the retained obligation cannot disappear into an identical candidate-only summary | SK-05, 08, 09 |\n| Q08 | Interrupt a bounded effect after its target changes but before acknowledgment; resume with the same work instruction | Reacquires the target and reconciles evidence before retry; no duplicated effect or claim that timeout proves rollback | SK-04, 08 |\n| Q09 | Give an old passing review, then materially change the governed source or candidate | Affected reliance is withdrawn and missing reassessment stays open; unrelated valid observations remain reusable | SK-07, 08, 09 |\n| Q10 | Exhaust construction before a candidate; perform an inconclusive assessment in another case; leave assessment unperformed in a third | Candidate availability, indeterminate assessment and no assessment are reported with the correct predicate and residual; no universal exhausted verdict | SK-08, 09 |\n| Q11 | Request release preparation with a local artifact grant and no remote publication grant; then separately exercise an authorized isolated local deployment | Exact reviewable candidate and destination; no unauthorized publication, no invented second final cut, verified local installed result on authorized path | SK-10 |\n| Q12 | Install, invoke and remove one tenant in a disposable host workspace; repeat after a compatible package update | Host discovers exact selected package, referenced files resolve, update identity is visible, removal is scoped and the other tenant/unrelated config remains intact | SK-01, 10, 11 |\n\n## Assessment record\n\nEach record binds case, lane, tenant/package and host/model identities, inputs and\nauthority, expected predicates, actual result and tool path, effect evidence,\nverdict per predicate, unresolved claims, measured/unknown costs and invalidators.\nSemantically assess unexpected behavior; a checker must not grade success solely\nfrom the presence of a named command or required phrase.\n\nInitial delivery claims each mandatory case separately. A failed or absent case\nwithholds that tenant's affected supported capability. The Executive may select a\nsmaller explicitly stated delivery claim only within the owner's outcome; it may\nnot erase a required capability by renaming a failed case optional.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9069a765-b3ee-4a03-8f71-6288ce3b57b6","uuid":"6d0e8367-c755-486c-a88a-8b294affa9ec","timestamp":"2026-10-25T02:06:17.493Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"===== specification/PRODUCT.md =====\n# STDO Skills\n\nStatus: common construction candidate under T-001; not Product acceptance or release.\n\n## Product boundary\n\nProduct identity: `urn:stdo:product-definition:stdo-skills`.\nBounded context: `urn:stdo-skills:bounded-context:agent-method-application`.\n\nThe Product supplies skills for F_P agents applying an exactly selected STDO\nmethodology to software-development work. It connects recognizable user tasks\nto the deciding source, appropriate context, supported tool operations and\nobservable returns. It owns requirements for this user-facing capability and\nevidence that each tenant realizes it.\n\nSTDO owns the method. a_c and other declared Development Products own their tool\ncontracts and computational implementations. A skill preserves and applies those\ncontracts; it does not create another lifecycle, state store or source of\nassessment, admission, acceptance or publication authority.\n\n## Selected methodology\n\nThe complete static builder and initial supported methodology is\n`stdo://releases/v3